speechtext:
This is Friday, and in the ordinary course Mr. Speaker would leave the chair without question put. I take it however that he asks unanimous consent for the purpose of considering the resolution. I move that the house resolve itself into committee of supply.
Motion agreed to and the house went into committee, Mr. Morand in the chair.
Mr. RHODES	moved:
Resolved that	a sum	not	exceeding
$16,058,114.05, being one-twelfth of the amount of each of the several items to be voted as set forth in the main estimates for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament, be granted to His Majesty, on account, for the fiscal year ending the 31st March, 1936.
Motion agreed	to.
Mr. RHODES	moved:
Resolved that	a sum	not	exceeding
$3,914,063, being one-sixth of the amount of each of the several following items to be voted, as set forth in the main estimates for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament, be granted to His Majesty, on account, for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, namely:
Votes Nos. 35, 36, 37 and 38, Legislation;
Vote No. 44, Experimental farms;
Vote No. 65, Pensions;
Vote No. 155, Geodetic survey (Control operations) ;
Vote No. 159, Fisheries;
Vote No. 163, Fish culture;
Vote No. 164, Oyster culture;
Vote No. 170, Geological survey;
Vote No. 185, Pay of force (R.C.M.P.);
Vote No. 193, Unemployment relief;
Vote No. 196, Sheltered employment;
Vote No. 226, Miscellaneous unforeseen expenses;
Vote No. 235, Chief Electoral Officer;
Vote No. 236, Dominion Franchise Commissioner;
Vote No. 267, National Revenue (Ports, Investigation, Audit and the Preventive Service, administration of the Income War Tax Act, 1917);
Vote No. 275, Trade and Commerce (Canadian coal);
Vote No. 277, Commercial Intelligence Service;
Vote No. 280, Electricity and Gas Inspection;
Vote No. 288, Weights and Measures.

As at August 31, 1934, they were: Nova Scotia, 2,282; Prince Edward Island, 754; New Brunswick, 3,491; Quebec, 51,337; Ontario, 21,126; Manitoba, 2,095;
Supply-National Defence

Saskatchewan, 2,669; Alberta, 1,792; British Columbia, 1,800. That makes a total of 87,346, a reduction of 46,986 compared with the year before.
